<p style=''font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;margin-top:6px;''>WTI settled near $69.40 on June 25, its fourth consecutive losing session. The toll-free shipping window under the June 17 US-Iran memorandum expires August 17. Source: Investing.com, Trading Economics.</p>
<p>Oil has fallen so far that almost no premium is left to absorb a shock if that deadline does not pass quietly. The same decline pulling Government of Canada yields toward a three-month low, the input the prescribed rate calculation depends on, also makes an August disruption a proportionally larger move than it would have been a month ago. The Bank of Canada, on the record this afternoon, is not treating either direction as settled. Advisors using this morning''s prescribed rate timing argument with clients should treat October as a base case, not a certainty, and keep August 17 on the calendar alongside it.</p>',
